{"name":"Carbon Copy","hex":"#545554","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"carbon copy","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A copy produced in an alternated stack of ordinary sheets of paper and carbon papers. The pressure applied on the top sheet (by a pen or typewriter) causes every carbon paper to release its carbon cover, thus reproducing the writing on the subjacent layers of paper.","A duplicate or lookalike."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To create a carbon copy of.","To send a duplicate copy of an email to."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/carbon_copy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Carbon copy","blocks":[{"text":"A carbon copy is a method of document copying where the original document is placed over a sheet of carbon paper and a blank sheet, then pressure is applied on the original document, so the document is printed on the blank sheet with carbon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Karbonkopia_2008.jpg/330px-Karbonkopia_2008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carbon_copy"},{"title":"Carbon Copy (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Carbon Copy is a 1981 comedy-drama film directed by Michael Schultz, produced by RKO Pictures and Hemdale Film Corporation, and released by Avco Embassy Pictures. The film stars George Segal, Susan Saint James, Jack Warden and Denzel Washington in his film acting debut. It was the first feature film produced by RKO after a break of many years."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/ca/Carbon_Copy_poster.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carbon_Copy_(film)"},{"title":"Carbon","blocks":[{"text":"Carbon is a chemical element; it has symbol C and atomic number 6. It is nonmetallic and tetravalent—meaning that its atoms are able to form up to four covalent bonds due to its valence shell exhibiting 4 electrons. It belongs to group 14 of the periodic table. Carbon makes up about 0.025 percent of Earth's crust. Three isotopes occur naturally, 12C and 13C being stable, while 14C is a radionuclide, decaying with a half-life of 5,700 years. Carbon is one of the few elements known since antiquity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Graphite-and-diamond-with-scale.jpg/330px-Graphite-and-diamond-with-scale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carbon"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/La_Libre_clandestine_15_Aout_1940_06.jpg/500px-La_Libre_clandestine_15_Aout_1940_06.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/La_Libre_clandestine_15_Aout_1940_06.jpg/1280px-La_Libre_clandestine_15_Aout_1940_06.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/La_Libre_clandestine_15_Aout_1940_06.jpg/500px-La_Libre_clandestine_15_Aout_1940_06.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"As creating carbon copies requires relatively few resources, it became a common method for producing underground and clandestine newspapers, as seen in this French example from World War II.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALa_Libre_clandestine_15_Aout_1940_06.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/LUNCHEON_%28held_by%29_TOWER_RESTAURANT_AND_CAFE_%28at%29_%22BLACKPOOL%2C_%28ENGLAND%29%22_%28FOR%3B_REST%3B%29_%28NYPL_Hades-274209-4000010340%29.tiff/lossy-page1-120px-LUNCHEON_%28held_by%29_TOWER_RESTAURANT_AND_CAFE_%28at%29_%22BLACKPOOL%2C_%28ENGLAND%29%22_%28FOR%3B_REST%3B%29_%28NYPL_Hades-274209-4000010340%29.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 